      "text": "To determine the probability that both the two-digit number \\(ab\\) (where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits from the two dice) and the digits \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) themselves are all divisible by 3, we first analyze the conditions for divisibility by 3.\n\nConsider each die:\n- The numbers 1 through 6 are: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\n- Divisible by 3 are 3 and 6: 3, 6.\n\nThus, the probability for each die that the outcome is divisible by 3 is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{divisible by 3}) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, since the dice are independent, the probability of both dice yielding outcomes divisible by 3 is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{both dice divisible by 3}) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{9}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(ab\\) such that both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3. We generate each section \\(n\\) such that:\n\\[\n3 \\leq n \\leq 6 \\text{ (the possible digits are 3, 4, 5, 6)}\n\\]\n\n- If \\(a = 3\\), the other 9 digits are 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 when multiplied.\n- If \\(a = 6\\), the other 5 digits are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 when multiplied.\n\nThus, \\(6\\) ways to select \\(a\\) either being \\(3\\) or \\(6\\), and:\n\\[\n3 \\times (3 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1) = 3 \\times 7 = 21\n\\]\n\nEach of the 10 possible multiple situations for \\(n, m\\) that satisfy the condition is uniformly distributed.\n\nWe pick one random trio \\((n, m)\\) out of 10 + other complex placements within the 100 possible outcomes. Here as \\(a\\) is 2, 3, 4 or 2.\n\\[\n\\text{Total set of pairs utilized:} \\, 21\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n21 \\times \\frac{1}{10} \\times \\frac{1}{9} \\, = \\frac{21}{90} = \\frac{7}{30}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final probability is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{30}}\n\\]",
